ont-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->We had to overprovision Aurora to handle our spiky traffic, and even then, the writer database would get overwhelmed. We provision 10x more than we need on average to keep things running smoothly.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Jonathan Reyes" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjonathan-reyes.04v9kv9idq6ay.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjonathan-reyes.04v9kv9idq6ay.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjonathan-reyes.04v9kv9idq6ay.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Jonathan Reyes</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">Principal Engineer at Dispatch</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<h2 id="your-traffic-isnt-flat-your-compute-shouldnt-be-either" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Your traffic isn’t flat, your compute shouldn’t be either</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#your-traffic-isnt-flat-your-compute-shouldnt-be-either" aria-label="Link to Your traffic isn’t flat, your compute shouldn’t be either"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>Most production workloads aren’t steady: they vary hour by hour, they drop at night, they dip on weekends; they spike further during migrations, bulk exports, product launches. The typical compute usage pattern of a production database looks like this:</p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Typical load pattern of a production database over one week" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Ftypical-weekly-load-pattern.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Ftypical-weekly-load-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Ftypical-weekly-load-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Typical load pattern of a production database over 24 hours" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Ftypical-daily-load-pattern.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Ftypical-daily-load-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Ftypical-daily-load-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<p><strong>There’s nothing fixed about this compute usage, yet most managed Postgres platforms still run on fixed-size instances.</strong></p>
<p>You’re forced to choose how much CPU and memory to buy up front, and end up locked into a structural tradeoff:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Overprovision to stay safe → waste money most of the time</strong></li>
<li><strong>Underprovision to save cost → risk performance degradation during spikes</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>Neon removes that tradeoff. <strong>Instead of locking your database into a fixed capacity, Neon automatically adjusts compute in real time to match your workload.</strong> Neon’s automatic sizing happens following an elaborate autoscaling algorithm that looks at multiple performance metrics and adjusts in near real-time. You don’t size instances, you don’t resize them later, you don’t guess - the platform does it for you.</p>
<div class="admonition not-prose my-9 rounded-none border-l-2 bg-gray-new-98 py-4 pr-5 pl-[1.125rem] dark:bg-gray-new-8 border-[#426CE0] dark:border-blue-70 [&amp;_pre]:px-4 [&amp;_pre]:py-3 [&amp;_pre_code]:text-sm! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:bg-white! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:dark:bg-gray-new-8!"><div class="flex items-center gap-1 text-[#426CE0] dark:text-blue-70"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="shrink-0"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M7.997 13.778a5.778 5.778 0 1 0 0-11.555 5.778 5.778 0 0 0 0 11.555"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 7.477v3.151M8 5.258v-.016"></path></svg><h4 class="font-mono text-[13px] leading-none font-medium -tracking-snug uppercase">info</h4></div><div class="admonition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [&amp;_a]:rounded-sm [&amp;_li]:text-base [&amp;_li]:leading-snug [&amp;_li]:tracking-extra-tight [&amp;_ul]:my-0 [&amp;_ol]:my-0"><p>Our <a class="" href="https://neon.com/autoscaling-report">platform data</a> shows that, when comparing production databases autoscaling in Neon to a provisioned model sized at P99.5 + 20%, 2.4x less compute is used, compute costs are around 50% lower on average, and around 55 resource exhaustion incidents per database per month are avoided.</p></div></div>
<h2 id="real-workloads-dont-respect-static-limits" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Real workloads don’t respect static limits</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#real-workloads-dont-respect-static-limits" aria-label="Link to Real workloads don’t respect static limits"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>To help engineers choose a database size, <strong>AWS provides a “rightsizing” recommendation: provision at P99.5 resource utilization + 20% buffer.</strong></p>
<p>In practice, this means:</p>
<ul>
<li>Look at your historical peak usage</li>
<li>Ignore the top 0.5% of load</li>
<li>Add 20% on top of that</li>
<li>Pay for that instance size</li>
</ul>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Recommended provisioned capacity vs true compute usage over 24 hours" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Frecommended-capacity-vs-true-usage.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Frecommended-capacity-vs-true-usage.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Frecommended-capacity-vs-true-usage.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<p>This method creates two inefficiencies:</p>
<ol>
<li>Most of the time, your database runs far below the capacity you’re paying for</li>
<li>The most extreme spikes still exceed that 20% buffer</li>
</ol>
<div class="admonition not-prose my-9 rounded-none border-l-2 bg-gray-new-98 py-4 pr-5 pl-[1.125rem] dark:bg-gray-new-8 border-[#426CE0] dark:border-blue-70 [&amp;_pre]:px-4 [&amp;_pre]:py-3 [&amp;_pre_code]:text-sm! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:bg-white! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:dark:bg-gray-new-8!"><div class="flex items-center gap-1 text-[#426CE0] dark:text-blue-70"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="shrink-0"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M7.997 13.778a5.778 5.778 0 1 0 0-11.555 5.778 5.778 0 0 0 0 11.555"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 7.477v3.151M8 5.258v-.016"></path></svg><h4 class="font-mono text-[13px] leading-none font-medium -tracking-snug uppercase">info</h4></div><div class="admonition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [&amp;_a]:rounded-sm [&amp;_li]:text-base [&amp;_li]:leading-snug [&amp;_li]:tracking-extra-tight [&amp;_ul]:my-0 [&amp;_ol]:my-0"><p>Our <a class="" href="https://neon.com/autoscaling-report">platform data</a> shows that, even when sized at P99.5 + 20%, the average production workload would exceed its provisioned capacity around 55 times per month.</p></div></div>
<p>To try to solve this loose-loose situation, you have two options.</p>
<h3 id="overprovision-or-buying-larger-instances-than-you-think-you-need" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Overprovision - or buying larger instances than you think you need</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#overprovision-or-buying-larger-instances-than-you-think-you-need" aria-label="Link to Overprovision - or buying larger instances than you think you need"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>This solves the potential performance degradation problem, but you end up with:</p>
<ul>
<li>Large areas of idle compute</li>
<li>Paying for capacity that delivers zero value</li>
<li>Higher steady-state infrastructure costs</li>
</ul>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Overprovisioned compute versus usage over 24 hours" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Foverprovisioned-compute-pattern.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Foverprovisioned-compute-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Foverprovisioned-compute-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<div class="admonition not-prose my-9 rounded-none border-l-2 bg-gray-new-98 py-4 pr-5 pl-[1.125rem] dark:bg-gray-new-8 border-[#426CE0] dark:border-blue-70 [&amp;_pre]:px-4 [&amp;_pre]:py-3 [&amp;_pre_code]:text-sm! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:bg-white! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:dark:bg-gray-new-8!"><div class="flex items-center gap-1 text-[#426CE0] dark:text-blue-70"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="shrink-0"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M7.997 13.778a5.778 5.778 0 1 0 0-11.555 5.778 5.778 0 0 0 0 11.555"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 7.477v3.151M8 5.258v-.016"></path></svg><h4 class="font-mono text-[13px] leading-none font-medium -tracking-snug uppercase">info</h4></div><div class="admonition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [&amp;_a]:rounded-sm [&amp;_li]:text-base [&amp;_li]:leading-snug [&amp;_li]:tracking-extra-tight [&amp;_ul]:my-0 [&amp;_ol]:my-0"><p>Our <a class="" href="https://neon.com/autoscaling-report">platform data</a> shows that the average production database would use 2.4x less compute under autoscaling than the equivalent P99.5 + 20% provisioned capacity. This difference is compute that would be paid for, but not used.</p></div></div>
<h3 id="underprovision-or-buying-smaller-instances-than-you-think-you-need" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Underprovision - or buying smaller instances than you think you need</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#underprovision-or-buying-smaller-instances-than-you-think-you-need" aria-label="Link to Underprovision - or buying smaller instances than you think you need"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>This reduces costs, but:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your CPU and memory get maxed out</li>
<li>You risk increasingly degraded performance or even outages</li>
</ul>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Underprovisioned compute versus usage over 24 hours" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Funderprovisioned-compute-pattern.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Funderprovisioned-compute-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Funderprovisioned-compute-pattern.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->[When we were using Heroku] we either had too many or too few resources allocated to our database, and adjustments typically were a big project with downtime.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Ben Halpern" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fben-halpern.10~cary~1x9te.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fben-halpern.10~cary~1x9te.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fben-halpern.10~cary~1x9te.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Ben Halpern</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">DEV Co-Founder</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<h2 id="compute-should-adapt-to-your-workload-not-the-other-way-around" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Compute should adapt to your workload, not the other way around</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#compute-should-adapt-to-your-workload-not-the-other-way-around" aria-label="Link to Compute should adapt to your workload, not the other way around"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p><strong>Provisioned databases require regularly making decisions on capacity tradeoffs. Neon removes that entire category of work.</strong></p>
<section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->Instead of having to overprovision our servers to handle peak loads, which leads to inefficiencies and higher costs, Neon&#x27;s autoscaling handles it. We get more performance when we need it.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Julian Benegas" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjulian-benegas.0ocrgiildwajz.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjulian-benegas.0ocrgiildwajz.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjulian-benegas.0ocrgiildwajz.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Julian Benegas</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">CEO of BaseHub</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<p>Instead of choosing a fixed instance size, Neon comes with autoscaling: it automatically allocates compute based on real-time workload demand. CPU, memory, and local file cache (LFC) are adjusted dynamically between a configured minimum and maximum. Compute simply follows your load in near real-time.</p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Neon autoscaling dynamically matches compute to workload" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Fneon-autoscaling-compute-allocation.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Fneon-autoscaling-compute-allocation.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Fvariable-load%2Fneon-autoscaling-compute-allocation.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
<h2 id="autoscaling-decisions-that-optimize-postgres-performance" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Autoscaling decisions that optimize Postgres performance</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#autoscaling-decisions-that-optimize-postgres-performance" aria-label="Link to Autoscaling decisions that optimize Postgres performance"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p><strong>Neon’s autoscaling isn’t a simple “CPU goes up → add more CPU” loop.</strong> Neon’s algorithm continuously evaluates multiple signals from the database and its underlying compute components to determine the optimal compute size at any moment.</p>
<section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->Scaling quickly used to be a challenge, but Neon gives us Postgres in a fully serverless setup. We don&#x27;t have to worry about provisioning or scaling, it just works.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="James Ross" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjames-ross.02sun54tva4h1.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjames-ross.02sun54tva4h1.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjames-ross.02sun54tva4h1.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">James Ross</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">Co-founder and CTO at Nodecraft</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<p>Neon’s autoscaling algorithm is built around three core metrics:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CPU load</strong> - sampled every 5 seconds, retrieving 1-minute averages</li>
<li><strong>Memory usage</strong> - the platform inspects Postgres-specific memory usage every 100 ms; every 5 seconds, the platform collects overall memory metrics from the underlying VM</li>
<li><strong>Local File Cache (LFC) working set size</strong> - every 20 seconds, the platform evaluates the working set size across multiple rolling windows (1-min, 2-min - up to 60 min). This is one of the most sophisticated datapoints collected by the Neon algorithm: at all times, it keeps the subset of data that is actively being accessed in memory to optimize performance.</li>
</ul>
<p>The platform’s algorithm has goals for each of these metrics, and it sizes compute accordingly to meet these goals - always bounded by your configured min and max autoscaling limits:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>“Keep the 1-minute average CPU load ≤ 90% of available CPU”</strong></li>
<li><strong>“Keep overall memory usage ≤ 75% of total RAM”</strong></li>
<li><strong>“Ensure the working set fits within 75% of RAM allocated to the Local File Cache”</strong></li>
</ul>
<div class="admonition not-prose my-9 rounded-none border-l-2 bg-gray-new-98 py-4 pr-5 pl-[1.125rem] dark:bg-gray-new-8 border-[#426CE0] dark:border-blue-70 [&amp;_pre]:px-4 [&amp;_pre]:py-3 [&amp;_pre_code]:text-sm! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:bg-white! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:dark:bg-gray-new-8!"><div class="flex items-center gap-1 text-[#426CE0] dark:text-blue-70"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="shrink-0"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M7.997 13.778a5.778 5.778 0 1 0 0-11.555 5.778 5.778 0 0 0 0 11.555"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 7.477v3.151M8 5.258v-.016"></path></svg><h4 class="font-mono text-[13px] leading-none font-medium -tracking-snug uppercase">info</h4></div><div class="admonition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [&amp;_a]:rounded-sm [&amp;_li]:text-base [&amp;_li]:leading-snug [&amp;_li]:tracking-extra-tight [&amp;_ul]:my-0 [&amp;_ol]:my-0"><p>The average production database on Neon <a class="" href="https://neon.com/autoscaling-report">adjusts its compute size 11,000+ times per month</a>. This demonstrates fine-grained scaling at runtime.</p></div></div>
<h2 id="non-production-databases-still-cost-money-but-far-less-with-scale-to-zero" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Non-production databases still cost money - but far less with scale-to-zero</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#non-production-databases-still-cost-money-but-far-less-with-scale-to-zero" aria-label="Link to Non-production databases still cost money - but far less with scale-to-zero"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>Autoscaling solves the “how much compute should I buy?” problem. Scale-to-zero solves a different one: <strong>Why are you paying for a database that isn’t being used?</strong></p>
<p>Your database setup isn’t just production - it includes development, testing, and staging environments too. These non-production databases don’t run continuously. But on provisioned platforms, even the smallest instance runs 24/7. You pay whether it’s active or not.</p>
<p><strong>On Neon, compute doesn’t just autoscale - it can shut down entirely when there are no active connections and resume in ~350ms.</strong> Non-production environments consume zero compute while idle and spin back up instantly when needed, without wasting mindspace on manually pausing/resuming and also without wasting costs</p>
<div class="admonition not-prose my-9 rounded-none border-l-2 bg-gray-new-98 py-4 pr-5 pl-[1.125rem] dark:bg-gray-new-8 border-[#426CE0] dark:border-blue-70 [&amp;_pre]:px-4 [&amp;_pre]:py-3 [&amp;_pre_code]:text-sm! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:bg-white! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:dark:bg-gray-new-8!"><div class="flex items-center gap-1 text-[#426CE0] dark:text-blue-70"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="shrink-0"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M7.997 13.778a5.778 5.778 0 1 0 0-11.555 5.778 5.778 0 0 0 0 11.555"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 7.477v3.151M8 5.258v-.016"></path></svg><h4 class="font-mono text-[13px] leading-none font-medium -tracking-snug uppercase">info</h4></div><div class="admonition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [&amp;_a]:rounded-sm [&amp;_li]:text-base [&amp;_li]:leading-snug [&amp;_li]:tracking-extra-tight [&amp;_ul]:my-0 [&amp;_ol]:my-0"><p>Our <a class="" href="https://neon.com/autoscaling-report">platform data</a> shows that non-production workloads that scale to zero use 13.7x less compute than their provisioned equivalent and cost 7.5x less.</p></div></div>
<h2 id="extra-serverless-postgres-perks" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Extra serverless Postgres perks</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#extra-serverless-postgres-perks" aria-label="Link to Extra serverless Postgres perks"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>When compute scales dynamically and can drop to zero, features built on top of it inherit those efficiencies. For example: read replicas and connection pooling become lighter, cheaper, and more responsive than their equivalents in provisioned infrastructure.</p>
<h3 id="read-replicas-dont-duplicate-budgets" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Read replicas don’t duplicate budgets</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#read-replicas-dont-duplicate-budgets" aria-label="Link to Read replicas don’t duplicate budgets"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->One of the big selling points of Neon was creating read replicas in seconds that scale to zero. We frequently spin up dev servers and sandbox environments, so providing easy, read-only access to the team while keeping maintenance low was a big win for us.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Jeremy Berman" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjeremy-berman.0r4ks52lw7zcf.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjeremy-berman.0r4ks52lw7zcf.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fjeremy-berman.0r4ks52lw7zcf.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Jeremy Berman</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">CTO at BeatGig</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<p>In provisioned platforms, each read replica runs its own compute instance, maintains its own memory allocation, and consumes steady-state capacity whether it’s actively serving traffic or not. This pretty much duplicates costs.</p>
<p>Neon’s read replicas work differently. <strong>Replicas share the same storage layer as the primary, autoscale independently, and scale to zero when inactive - they’re significantly more efficient.</strong> You can freely use them for analytics workloads, business dashboards, heavy read queries, or read-only access. They’re also instantly available as soon as they’re deployed.</p>
<div class="admonition not-prose my-9 rounded-none border-l-2 bg-gray-new-98 py-4 pr-5 pl-[1.125rem] dark:bg-gray-new-8 border-[#426CE0] dark:border-blue-70 [&amp;_pre]:px-4 [&amp;_pre]:py-3 [&amp;_pre_code]:text-sm! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:bg-white! [&amp;_pre[data-language]]:dark:bg-gray-new-8!"><div class="flex items-center gap-1 text-[#426CE0] dark:text-blue-70"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 16" class="shrink-0"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M7.997 13.778a5.778 5.778 0 1 0 0-11.555 5.778 5.778 0 0 0 0 11.555"></path><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 7.477v3.151M8 5.258v-.016"></path></svg><h4 class="font-mono text-[13px] leading-none font-medium -tracking-snug uppercase">info</h4></div><div class="admonition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [&amp;_a]:rounded-sm [&amp;_li]:text-base [&amp;_li]:leading-snug [&amp;_li]:tracking-extra-tight [&amp;_ul]:my-0 [&amp;_ol]:my-0"><p>Our <a class="" href="https://neon.com/autoscaling-report">platform data</a> shows that Neon read replicas use 4x less compute than their provisioned equivalent and cost around 78% less.</p></div></div>
<h3 id="connection-pooling-scales-with-your-workload" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>Connection pooling scales with your workload</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#connection-pooling-scales-with-your-workload" aria-label="Link to Connection pooling scales with your workload"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->Neon worked out of the box, handling hundreds of Lambdas without any of the connection issues we saw in Aurora Serverless v2. On top of that, Neon costs us 1/6 of what we were paying with AWS.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-full"><img alt="Cody Jenkins" loading="lazy" width="32" height="32" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="pointer-events-none m-0 size-8 rounded-full object-cover sm:size-7" style="color:transparent" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fcody-jenkins.12-u~sf5u8n1d.jpg&amp;w=32&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fcody-jenkins.12-u~sf5u8n1d.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Fcody-jenkins.12-u~sf5u8n1d.jpg&amp;w=64&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/></div><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Cody Jenkins</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">Head of Engineering at Invenco</cite></div></div></figcaption></figure></section>
<p>Traffic spikes don’t just increase CPU and memory pressure, they also increase connection pressure. Provisioned Postgres instances have hard limits on <code>max_connections</code> - exceed them, and new connections are rejected. Even adding a proxy like RDS Proxy introduces queueing, latency, and operational complexity.</p>
<p>In Neon, connection pooling is built directly into every Neon endpoint. By simply using the pooled connection string (adding <code>-pooler</code>), connections are routed through PgBouncer in transaction pooling mode, which gives you up to 10,000 concurrent client connections.</p>
<p>Pooling and autoscaling work together in Neon. As connection pressure increases memory and CPU usage, Neon’s autoscaling algorithm reacts, increasing compute resources instead of failing requests. Requests are queued instead of rejected, and connections are reused aggressively.</p>
